financially and we're poor.

00:02:21.242 --> 00:02:26.242
So, this is from (laughs)
Mike Walterret on Patreon.

00:02:29.575 --> 00:02:30.737
And this is what he says,

00:02:30.737 --> 00:02:33.790
"On shows like The
Orville and many others,

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:38.140
they often communicate with
people light years away.

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:40.340
They never explain how they do it.

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:42.770
Maybe there's a wire that
passes through a tiny wormhole

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:44.710
to connect earth.

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:48.230
What I'm wondering though
is, if they were able

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:50.800
to reliably share the same sense of time

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:53.290
for the duration of their conversation,

00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:55.803
would one of them sound like a chipmunk

00:02:55.803 --> 00:02:59.260
and the others sound like
they're in slow motion.

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:01.470
What other weird effects might occur?"

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:02.380
- That's a great question.

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:03.603
- That's a really great question.

00:03:03.603 --> 00:03:05.220
- Because we are so distracted by that,

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:07.310
it wouldn't make noise
in the vacuum of space.

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:08.143
- [Nice] Right.

00:03:08.143 --> 00:03:09.428
- And this person is thinking
deep about our conversations.

00:03:09.428 --> 00:03:10.620
- [Nice] Yes he is.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:12.570
We have this issue with communicating

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:14.200
with the Mars rover.
- Right.

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:15.033
- The Mars rover.

00:03:15.033 --> 00:03:15.866
- What is the delay on the Mars rover?

00:03:15.866 --> 00:03:17.520
- It's on average about 20 minutes.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:19.300
- So is it so you, "Hello?"

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:20.860
- Watch out for the
cliff, and it's too late.

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:22.200
- Right, so you gotta
make sure, (laughing)

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:23.983
watch out for the cliff, Jesus.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:28.300
- That's why the rovers
all have some kind of A.I.

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:29.840
on them to know where they're heading

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:31.336
and how dangerous it might be.

00:03:31.336 --> 00:03:32.169
- Right.

00:03:32.169 --> 00:03:33.480
- Regardless of what command we give them.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:38.253
- Right, it's like being a
lousy parent at the playground.

00:03:38.253 --> 00:03:39.190
(laughing)

00:03:39.190 --> 00:03:40.653
You know what I mean?

00:03:42.141 --> 00:03:44.590
- So, clearly they're
communicating with people

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:46.890
much farther away than just Earth to Mars.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:47.723
- [Nice] Right.

00:03:47.723 --> 00:03:51.350
- So, I'm thinking its gotta
be some wormhole channel.

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:52.920
Charles, you got an opinion about that?

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:54.940
- In Star Trek specifically,

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:56.730
there is this construct called subspace.

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:58.180
- Subspace, yeah.

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:00.433
- Which transcends regular space and time.

00:04:00.433 --> 00:04:03.200
And basically anything
that happens in subspace,

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:05.670
you can just assume it works just as if

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:07.720
you and I were next door to each other,

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:08.730
or in the same room.

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:09.710
- In real time.

00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:12.970
- Yes, but that completely doesn't affect

00:04:12.970 --> 00:04:14.903
the entire rest of space and time.

00:04:14.903 --> 00:04:16.740
- It's really convenient.
- It's really a false,

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:18.750
entertaining, convenient construct.

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:20.241
- It'd be cool if that
were a thing, right?

00:04:20.241 --> 00:04:21.710
- Yeah, right, it would,

00:04:21.710 --> 00:04:22.797
and perhaps some day that will be--

00:04:22.797 --> 00:04:24.190
- But if that were the case--

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:25.118
- Wait, wait, wait, wait.

00:04:25.118 --> 00:04:29.033
So, (laughing) Verizon
Fios, eat your heart out.

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:31.390
- The problem of course is that,

00:04:31.390 --> 00:04:35.310
if you can have this
transluminal communication,

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:37.120
you really mess up causality.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:38.220
You could really have an issue.

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:41.310
Like, let's say I
somehow am magically able

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:44.750
to tell that Mars rover,
"Watch out for the cliff,"

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:49.750
in instantaneous time, then
the rest of the signal,

00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:52.160
somebody else is watching that rover.

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:54.570
It takes 20 minutes for us to see that

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:57.140
that rover avoided the cliff.

00:04:57.140 --> 00:05:00.490
And so, you're really turning
into a strange opportunity

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:04.910
to twist what's causing
what, who's causing things,

00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:07.720
and all of physics starts to break down

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:08.960
under those circumstances.

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:10.550
So, superluminal communication,

00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:12.400
or faster than light communication

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:17.150
is approximately as challenging
as faster than light travel

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:20.360
to our ability to understand
how physics works.

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:23.090
- And in fact, if this
is just the movement

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:26.300
of information, it has
tremendous consequences

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:28.620
if not done according to the universe

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:31.569
in which we have laid
down our laws of physics.

00:05:31.569 --> 00:05:32.547
- [Liu] Yeah, right.

00:05:32.547 --> 00:05:35.420
- But is there a coherent
set of laws of physics

00:05:35.420 --> 00:05:37.910
that will allow superluminal communication

00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:38.970
and not mess things up?

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:40.320
- At the moment, no.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:41.820
- So lets say, if we're
waiting for someone else

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:43.110
to be born--
- Pretty much.

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:46.223
Einstein Junior, all right,
take us to the next step.

00:05:46.223 --> 00:05:49.270
- Little Einstein.
- That's right, yeah.

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:51.470
So the comments about whether or not

00:05:51.470 --> 00:05:56.160
you sound like a chipmunk or
you talk really slowly, is

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:57.090
- [Tyson] At low frequency.

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:57.923
- Yes, is valid--

00:05:57.923 --> 00:05:59.060
- [Tyson] You could speak
slowly at a high frequency.

00:05:59.060 --> 00:05:59.927
- That's right.

00:05:59.927 --> 00:06:02.773
Look, I speak slowly
at the high frequency.

00:06:02.773 --> 00:06:04.207
Sorry, but the idea is--

00:06:04.207 --> 00:06:05.688
- You know my favorite scene ever?

00:06:05.688 --> 00:06:06.661
(laughing)

00:06:06.661 --> 00:06:11.661
It was in, what's that
Little Red Riding Hood movie?

00:06:12.980 --> 00:06:14.002
Hoodwinked!
- [Nice] Hoodwinked.

00:06:14.002 --> 00:06:16.680
- In Hoodwinked, the chipmunk,

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:18.715
no one understood the chipmunk.

00:06:18.715 --> 00:06:20.980
The chipmunk was like (imitates chipmunk),

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:22.897
and you just think it's just
being a cute little chipmunk

00:06:22.897 --> 00:06:24.217
and then the detective says,

00:06:24.217 --> 00:06:25.730
"I think I know what's going on here."

00:06:25.730 --> 00:06:27.624
He records the chipmunk, then
plays it back in slow motion.

00:06:27.624 --> 00:06:29.812
- And then slows it down.
- Right.

00:06:29.812 --> 00:06:32.304
- Plays it back and it
says, "I was witness to--"

00:06:32.304 --> 00:06:33.600
(laughing)

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:37.140
It's this slow, deliberate,
low frequency voice.

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:37.973
- That's cool.

00:06:37.973 --> 00:06:39.110
- Translating the chipmunk.

00:06:39.110 --> 00:06:41.710
- Right, so that kind of effect

00:06:41.710 --> 00:06:43.670
is what we call the doppler effect.

00:06:43.670 --> 00:06:44.503
- Right.

00:06:44.503 --> 00:06:45.560
- Especially the doppler effect for sound

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:47.508
and Neil, you and I
understand that very well.

00:06:47.508 --> 00:06:50.510
Just when we're out on
the streets in New York,

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:52.703
