What do we call them?

00:16:42.433 --> 00:16:43.360
Geekspertise.

00:16:43.360 --> 00:16:44.495
- Geekspertise.

00:16:44.495 --> 00:16:47.170
- Yes, he's our resident Geek in Chief.

00:16:47.170 --> 00:16:48.140
There it is.

00:16:48.140 --> 00:16:53.070
That these are things you might
watch while eating popcorn.

00:16:53.070 --> 00:16:54.960
Pop fiction.

00:16:54.960 --> 00:16:56.632
- Made in your science oven!

00:16:56.632 --> 00:16:59.070
(laughing)

00:16:59.070 --> 00:17:00.300
- By the way, I think popcorn

00:17:00.300 --> 00:17:03.130
is one of the most
extraordinary foods ever.

00:17:03.130 --> 00:17:03.963
- [Liu] Right.

00:17:03.963 --> 00:17:05.397
- How many things do you say,

00:17:05.397 --> 00:17:07.480
"Gee, I want to turn that inside out

00:17:07.480 --> 00:17:08.313
and then eat it.
- [Liu] Right,

00:17:08.313 --> 00:17:09.500
and then eat it.

00:17:09.500 --> 00:17:11.125
- Just every cow I see.

00:17:11.125 --> 00:17:12.150
(heavy laughter)

00:17:12.150 --> 00:17:13.623
I mean, but that's just me!

00:17:15.590 --> 00:17:18.960
- Okay, all right, technically
you're kinda doing that.

00:17:18.960 --> 00:17:22.540
But just if you look at
a kernel in one instant

00:17:22.540 --> 00:17:25.760
and then the next instant
its completely inside out.

00:17:25.760 --> 00:17:28.230
And if you didn't tell someone
what had just happened,

00:17:28.230 --> 00:17:29.840
they would think its two
completely different foods.

00:17:29.840 --> 00:17:31.370
- [Nice] See, you're absolutely right.

00:17:31.370 --> 00:17:32.203
- That's my only point.

00:17:32.203 --> 00:17:33.320
- There's nobody who
could look at a kernel

00:17:33.320 --> 00:17:35.425
and then look at a
popcorn, like flourished,

00:17:35.425 --> 00:17:38.100
and say, "Oh, that came from that."

00:17:38.100 --> 00:17:40.100
- And it was specifically
designed by nature

00:17:40.100 --> 00:17:41.710
to be absorbent to butter. (chuckles)

00:17:41.710 --> 00:17:43.960
(laughing)

00:17:45.277 --> 00:17:46.380
Okay, so its the perfect food.

00:17:46.380 --> 00:17:48.480
- It is.
- And almost no calories.

00:17:48.480 --> 00:17:49.790
- Oh yes, well air!

00:17:49.790 --> 00:17:51.920
So you gotta load up the butter calories

00:17:51.920 --> 00:17:52.860
otherwise, what are you doing?

00:17:52.860 --> 00:17:55.740
Yeah I tried eating air
popped popcorn without butter.

00:17:55.740 --> 00:17:56.573
Its like, what am I doing?

00:17:56.573 --> 00:17:57.722
- I kinda like it.

00:17:57.722 --> 00:17:58.734
- [Tyson] Nah, you're lying.

00:17:58.734 --> 00:18:00.334
- The question is, does it taco?

00:18:01.200 --> 00:18:02.703
- [Tyson] Does it what?
- What?

00:18:02.703 --> 00:18:03.727
- You guys don't know this.
- Does it taco?

00:18:03.727 --> 00:18:06.063
- Yeah, it's just this
young generation thing.

00:18:07.320 --> 00:18:10.590
Can you put this particular
food into a taco shell

00:18:10.590 --> 00:18:11.423
and eat it?

00:18:11.423 --> 00:18:12.646
- Oh!
- Does it actually taste good?

00:18:12.646 --> 00:18:13.717
- So, taco is a verb?

00:18:13.717 --> 00:18:15.580
- Yeah, does it taco?
- Does it taco?

00:18:15.580 --> 00:18:16.870
Yes, does popcorn?

00:18:16.870 --> 00:18:17.703
- Oh, taco.

00:18:17.703 --> 00:18:18.536
- Why not?

00:18:18.536 --> 00:18:19.690
It could fit many things.
- Definitely taco,

00:18:19.690 --> 00:18:20.640
especially if you put a sauce on it!

00:18:20.640 --> 00:18:22.300
- You know what else?

00:18:22.300 --> 00:18:24.880
Styrofoam also tacos.

00:18:24.880 --> 00:18:27.010
Packing Peanuts.
Now how somebody knows that,

00:18:27.010 --> 00:18:27.843
I don't even want to know.

00:18:27.843 --> 00:18:28.940
- You don't even want to ask.

00:18:28.940 --> 00:18:29.773
- Don't even want to ask!

00:18:29.773 --> 00:18:30.636
- Yeah, don't even, don't go there.

00:18:30.636 --> 00:18:31.991
- How do you even know
that styrofoam tacos?

00:18:31.991 --> 00:18:34.265
- The edible kind, not
the polystyrene stuff.

00:18:34.265 --> 00:18:38.653
- Oh, you mean the starch popcorn.

00:18:38.653 --> 00:18:41.147
- Yeah the starch based
popcorns that don't kill you

00:18:41.147 --> 00:18:42.663
if you eat them.
- Right.

00:18:43.559 --> 00:18:44.630
- Plus, you can put them I the toilet

00:18:44.630 --> 00:18:45.463
and they just dissolve.

00:18:45.463 --> 00:18:46.490
- Right, they're designed to disintegrate.

00:18:46.490 --> 00:18:47.620
Do they taco?

00:18:47.620 --> 00:18:49.080
It turns out, they do.

00:18:49.080 --> 00:18:49.913
How about that.

00:18:49.913 --> 00:18:50.746
The same is true with popcorn

00:18:50.746 --> 00:18:52.260
- Is dissolving the
same as disintegrating,

00:18:52.260 --> 00:18:53.710
I don't think so.

00:18:53.710 --> 00:18:54.733
They just dissolve in water.

00:18:54.733 --> 00:18:58.000
- Dissolution is a form of disintegration.

00:18:58.000 --> 00:18:58.833
- Ill give it to you.

00:18:58.833 --> 00:18:59.940
- [Liu] There you go, okay.

00:18:59.940 --> 00:19:00.773
- Nicely done.

00:19:00.773 --> 00:19:01.910
(laughing)

00:19:01.910 --> 00:19:04.860
I gotta tell ya, these
guys make eating a taco

00:19:06.020 --> 00:19:07.510
that much more exciting to listen to.

00:19:07.510 --> 00:19:09.307
- It is the joy of the geekiverse.

00:19:09.307 --> 00:19:10.190
- Wait, wait, wait.

00:19:10.190 --> 00:19:13.470
How would you know in advance
if you were a three year old,

00:19:13.470 --> 00:19:17.540
whether you're eating the
polystyrene looking corn

00:19:17.540 --> 00:19:19.514
or the starch looking corn.

00:19:19.514 --> 00:19:21.190
- I have the answer to that.

00:19:21.190 --> 00:19:22.878
When you don't end up in the hospital,

00:19:22.878 --> 00:19:23.790
(heavy laughter)

00:19:23.790 --> 00:19:25.620
you ate the right one.

00:19:25.620 --> 00:19:26.453
- Thank you, Chuck.

00:19:26.453 --> 00:19:28.503
- Biodegrades, as opposed
to the stuff that doesn't.

00:19:28.503 --> 00:19:29.739
- Thank you Chuck.

00:19:29.739 --> 00:19:31.090
All right, give us another question.

00:19:31.090 --> 00:19:31.923
- All right, here we go.

00:19:31.923 --> 00:19:34.280
Lets get back to our queries

00:19:34.280 --> 00:19:36.940
that come from all over the internet.

00:19:36.940 --> 00:19:40.380
This is Bellamariani.

00:19:40.380 --> 00:19:42.165
Bellamariani says--

00:19:42.165 --> 00:19:43.273
- Is it Bella?

00:19:43.273 --> 00:19:45.241
- Its actually all one.

00:19:45.241 --> 00:19:46.305
- Oh Bellamoroni, one word.

00:19:46.305 --> 00:19:47.138
- Bellamariani, yeah it's Bellamariani.

00:19:50.615 --> 00:19:51.597
- Oh Mariani.

00:19:51.597 --> 00:19:52.430
- Bellmariani.

00:19:52.430 --> 00:19:55.180
- Because Bella Moroni is
like a beautiful moron.

00:19:55.180 --> 00:19:56.013
- Right.

00:19:56.013 --> 00:19:57.480
(laughing)

00:19:57.480 --> 00:20:00.870
- However, moroni is
Swahili for warrior as well.

00:20:00.870 --> 00:20:02.269
- Excuse me.

00:20:02.269 --> 00:20:03.274
- But not with the Bella on it.

00:20:03.274 --> 00:20:04.871
(laughing)

00:20:04.871 --> 00:20:05.704
- All right, what do ya got?

00:20:05.704 --> 00:20:07.037
- All right, here we go,

00:20:07.037 --> 00:20:09.450
"Hey Chuck, will it one day be possible

00:20:09.450 --> 00:20:12.540
for entire societies
to disguise themselves

00:20:12.540 --> 00:20:16.428
in plain sight as Wakanda
did in the Marvel universe.

00:20:16.428 --> 00:20:21.428
How close are we to developing
invisibility technology?

00:20:22.610 --> 00:20:25.530
- So lets get the top
presentations of that.

00:20:25.530 --> 00:20:27.637
So you have the cloaking
device on Star Trek

00:20:27.637 --> 00:20:30.680
and also one of the James
Bond movies with Daniel Craig.

00:20:30.680 --> 00:20:32.530
I forgot which one it was called,

00:20:32.530 --> 00:20:35.120
but it has something
like adaptive camouflage.

00:20:35.120 --> 00:20:39.062
So, you can park it and
whatever it was in front of,

00:20:39.062 --> 00:20:42.010
it would bring that pattern to the car

00:20:42.010 --> 00:20:43.660
and you'd walk by it and you
just thought you were looking

00:20:43.660 --> 00:20:45.070
at a normal scene.

00:20:45.070 --> 00:20:46.320
So there's that.

00:20:46.320 --> 00:20:51.320
There was also cloaking in Chicken Little.

00:20:51.486 --> 00:20:52.930
(laughing)
- Okay.

00:20:52.930 --> 00:20:56.980
- Excuse me, is my cinematic
repertoire to large for you?

00:20:56.980 --> 00:20:57.863
- Probably so!

00:20:58.880 --> 00:21:00.060
Anytime you're referencing Chicken Little!

00:21:00.060 --> 00:21:01.430
- Okay, so sky--

00:21:01.430 --> 00:21:02.263
- is falling.

00:21:02.263 --> 00:21:03.610
- Was not a real sky.

00:21:03.610 --> 00:21:06.660
They were hexagonal tiles
that the aliens had put

00:21:06.660 --> 00:21:11.660
over the earth, and the
tile is an exact image

00:21:11.660 --> 00:21:13.320
of what is behind it.

00:21:13.320 --> 00:21:15.490
And when a tile fell down,

00:21:15.490 --> 00:21:19.340
it fell to the ground, and
Chicken Little looked at it

00:21:19.340 --> 00:21:23.142
and touched it and it immediately
became the floorboards.

00:21:23.142 --> 00:21:23.975
(wowing)

00:21:23.975 --> 00:21:25.710
And he put it on the table
and it became the table!

00:21:25.710 --> 00:21:28.050
And so, there's five
minutes of this movie,

00:21:28.050 --> 00:21:31.040
doing experiments with
the hexagonal tiles.

00:21:31.040 --> 00:21:31.873
- Love it.

00:21:31.873 --> 00:21:33.850
- So basically, its cloaking
by not becoming invisible,

00:21:33.850 --> 00:21:35.670
but by becoming what is behind it.

00:21:35.670 --> 00:21:36.503
- Camouflage.

00:21:36.503 --> 00:21:37.850
- Camouflage, perfect camouflage.

00:21:37.850 --> 00:21:39.260
So that's one kind of invisibility.

00:21:39.260 --> 00:21:40.093
- Sure.

00:21:40.093 --> 00:21:41.710
- And then you have, what else?

00:21:41.710 --> 00:21:43.135
- Un-notice ability
- Don't forget.

00:21:43.135 --> 00:21:44.420
- [Tyson] Un-notice ability.

00:21:44.420 --> 00:21:45.857
As opposed to--
- That's right

00:21:45.857 --> 00:21:46.900
- So, Harry Potter.

00:21:46.900 --> 00:21:48.698
- [Tyson] Charles is
inventing words today.

00:21:48.698 --> 00:21:50.110
- I can't take credit for that.

00:21:50.110 --> 00:21:51.120
- [Tyson] Okay, so the cloak.

00:21:51.120 --> 00:21:51.970
- The cloak in Harry Potter.

00:21:51.970 --> 00:21:53.280
- The cloak in Harry Potter.

00:21:53.280 --> 00:21:57.480
You have the Invisible Woman,
the Fantastic Four character

00:21:57.480 --> 00:21:58.740
who can turn invisible

00:21:58.740 --> 00:22:01.262
- She can turn invisible as well as the--

00:22:01.262 --> 00:22:02.095
- Well there's

00:22:02.095 --> 00:22:02.957
the Invisible Man.
- The Incredibles.

00:22:02.957 --> 00:22:04.180
The original Invisible Man.

00:22:04.180 --> 00:22:05.770
- [Nice] And the original Invisible Man.

00:22:05.770 --> 00:22:08.560
So what gets me is, just
because he's invisible

00:22:08.560 --> 00:22:11.760
shouldn't automatically mean
he can walk through walls.

00:22:11.760 --> 00:22:12.593
- No, he never walked through walls.

00:22:12.593 --> 00:22:13.770
- Why should is mean that at all?

00:22:13.770 --> 00:22:14.603
- What do you mean?

00:22:14.603 --> 00:22:15.436
- Yes, of course he did!

00:22:15.436 --> 00:22:17.670
- H.G. Wells Invisible Man
didn't walk through walls.

00:22:17.670 --> 00:22:19.020
- No, the movie.
- He had to walk naked the--

00:22:19.020 --> 00:22:21.990
No, I'm talking about the guy
who was wrapped in a thing

00:22:21.990 --> 00:22:23.550
and they unwrapped it
and he was invisible.

00:22:23.550 --> 00:22:25.050
- Yeah, he couldn't walk through walls.

00:22:25.050 --> 00:22:25.883
- He couldn't walk through walls?

00:22:25.883 --> 00:22:26.716
- No.

00:22:26.716 --> 00:22:27.549
- You sure?

00:22:27.549 --> 00:22:28.382
- Not the original

00:22:28.382 --> 00:22:29.215
Invisible Man.
- I'm too young

00:22:29.215 --> 00:22:30.048
for this conversation.

00:22:30.048 --> 00:22:30.881
(laughs loudly)

00:22:30.881 --> 00:22:32.130
- No, no, no.

00:22:32.130 --> 00:22:33.540
His problem was also that he had to--

00:22:33.540 --> 00:22:35.658
- Oh I know, I'm confusing him with ghosts

00:22:35.658 --> 00:22:36.491
who go through walls.

00:22:36.491 --> 00:22:38.820
- Oh, yeah, well movie ghosts, yeah.

00:22:38.820 --> 00:22:40.220
- Why can you walk through
walls, but not fall

00:22:40.220 --> 00:22:41.053
into the basement?

00:22:41.053 --> 00:22:41.886
(chuckles)

00:22:41.886 --> 00:22:42.719
They almost leave that out.

00:22:42.719 --> 00:22:43.552
- [Liu] Right.

00:22:43.552 --> 00:22:44.385
- Right.

00:22:44.385 --> 00:22:45.218
- Why is the floor holding you up,

00:22:45.218 --> 00:22:46.870
but you can walk through a wall.

00:22:46.870 --> 00:22:47.703
- [Nice] Interesting.

00:22:47.703 --> 00:22:48.536
- It doesn't make any sense.

00:22:48.536 --> 00:22:49.400
Okay, so who else do we have?

00:22:49.400 --> 00:22:50.233
Invisible Woman.

00:22:50.233 --> 00:22:52.480
- Invisible Woman, we got
the Harry Potter thing,

00:22:52.480 --> 00:22:55.480
we got the Star Trek as you already said.

00:22:55.480 --> 00:22:57.090
- Cloaking devices.
- Cloaking devices.

00:22:57.090 --> 00:22:59.620
- And then, you have the
entire city being cloaked.

00:22:59.620 --> 00:23:01.920
- [Tyson] Yes, so can
we cloak an entire city?

00:23:01.920 --> 00:23:04.060
- Well in Wakanda yeah, they did that.

00:23:04.060 --> 00:23:06.820
- Well, the answer at the moment is--

00:23:06.820 --> 00:23:08.960
- No! (chuckles)

00:23:08.960 --> 00:23:10.065
I'm guessing--

00:23:10.065 --> 00:23:11.881
- We can't do a city.

00:23:11.881 --> 00:23:12.877
Going out on a limb.
- I'm betting.

00:23:12.877 --> 00:23:15.034
- Chuck, lets bet on that one.

00:23:15.034 --> 00:23:16.210
- Yeah, can't do a city.

00:23:16.210 --> 00:23:18.950
- Chuck, can we cloak a whole city?

00:23:18.950 --> 00:23:22.920
- No, at the moment the
answer is that we can seem

00:23:22.920 --> 00:23:26.580
to be able to bend light
around objects in such a way

00:23:26.580 --> 00:23:28.820
that the object would not be visible

00:23:28.820 --> 00:23:30.790
to someone looking at it
because all they would see

00:23:30.790 --> 00:23:31.623
is the background.

00:23:31.623 --> 00:23:33.553
- And you don't even know
the light is bending,

00:23:33.553 --> 00:23:35.890
you just see the direction
the light comes from

00:23:35.890 --> 00:23:36.960
at the last point of contact.

00:23:36.960 --> 00:23:37.793
- That's right.

00:23:37.793 --> 00:23:42.793
- So the light can do a full
360, 180, it doesn't matter

00:23:42.850 --> 00:23:45.470
and you look, it all the light

00:23:45.470 --> 00:23:47.857
coherently comes around the object.

00:23:47.857 --> 00:23:50.700
Again, you've rendered it invisible.

00:23:50.700 --> 00:23:53.280
- That is, at this moment, only doable

00:23:53.280 --> 00:23:56.870
in the laboratory under
very extreme conditions

00:23:56.870 --> 00:23:58.390
sort of at the subatomic level.

00:23:58.390 --> 00:24:00.040
- Okay, however Charles--

00:24:00.040 --> 00:24:03.526
- But I thought Darpa was
working on a cloak like that.

00:24:03.526 --> 00:24:04.359
- That's the kind--

00:24:04.359 --> 00:24:05.440
- Is that it?

00:24:05.440 --> 00:24:06.273
Okay.

00:24:06.273 --> 00:24:07.348
- I'm not authorized to speak any further.

00:24:07.348 --> 00:24:08.880
(laughing)

00:24:08.880 --> 00:24:10.417
- But Charles, here's the problem.

00:24:10.417 --> 00:24:12.680
The city is sitting on the ground.

00:24:12.680 --> 00:24:13.550
- [Liu] Yes.

00:24:13.550 --> 00:24:16.533
You can't bring light from
behind the city around it.

00:24:17.700 --> 00:24:18.534
- [Liu] Right.

00:24:18.534 --> 00:24:22.663
- So that technology
wouldn't work to hide a city.

00:24:24.476 --> 00:24:26.010
It would only hide objects
in front of other things.

00:24:26.010 --> 00:24:29.540
- In Wakanda they had, it was a dome

00:24:29.540 --> 00:24:31.640
that covered the city as well.

00:24:31.640 --> 00:24:34.120
There was some kind of like, force dome

00:24:34.120 --> 00:24:36.060
that then cloaked the city.

00:24:36.060 --> 00:24:38.600
- Right, the dome in
that case was really just

00:24:38.600 --> 00:24:39.660
almost a mirror.

00:24:39.660 --> 00:24:42.450
In a sense it projected upon itself.

00:24:42.450 --> 00:24:45.785
Think pastoral images of
goat herders and sheep.

00:24:45.785 --> 00:24:46.618
- Okay, so it didn't disappear.

00:24:46.618 --> 00:24:48.071
It's just a projection screen.

00:24:48.071 --> 00:24:49.298
- Its just a projection screen,

00:24:49.298 --> 00:24:50.810
and then you had to get through it

00:24:50.810 --> 00:24:53.710
and then you saw all the
marvelous technology.

00:24:53.710 --> 00:24:54.543
- Cool.

00:24:54.543 --> 00:24:55.376
- So that we can do.

00:24:55.376 --> 00:24:57.530
We're already projecting
movies on to skyscrapers,

00:24:57.530 --> 00:24:58.363
aren't we?

00:24:58.363 --> 00:24:59.196
- You know what got me about Wakanda?

00:24:59.196 --> 00:25:01.250
How is it that down in the town,

00:25:01.250 --> 00:25:05.000
this is with all this
technology and all this stuff.

00:25:05.000 --> 00:25:08.451
They're still selling woven
baskets in the street.

00:25:08.451 --> 00:25:09.460
What's up with that.

00:25:09.460 --> 00:25:11.210
- I believe it's very simple.

00:25:11.210 --> 00:25:12.043
- What?

00:25:12.043 --> 00:25:15.120
- Because they realize they
essentially have infinite wealth

00:25:15.120 --> 00:25:17.380
and infinite technology.

00:25:17.380 --> 00:25:18.510
What makes them happy?

00:25:18.510 --> 00:25:19.564
Its not the toys.

00:25:19.564 --> 00:25:20.397
- Baskets!

00:25:20.397 --> 00:25:21.886
(laughing)
- It's baskets!

00:25:21.886 --> 00:25:23.360
- Baskets!

00:25:23.360 --> 00:25:25.760
I totally get that.
- Straw baskets!

00:25:25.760 --> 00:25:28.651
I would much rather, like, make a basket,

00:25:28.651 --> 00:25:29.760
enjoy that basket.
- Look at the workmanship

00:25:29.760 --> 00:25:30.700
on this basket!
(chuckling)

00:25:30.700 --> 00:25:32.860
- Then like, you know, point my finger

00:25:32.860 --> 00:25:35.000
and I have this sort of virtual basket

00:25:35.000 --> 00:25:37.480
made of vibranium that's gonna--

00:25:37.480 --> 00:25:39.068
- I want a basket made of vibranium.

00:25:39.068 --> 00:25:39.901
That's what I want.

00:25:39.901 --> 00:25:42.010
- Well I would appreciate
that too if it were artistic

00:25:42.010 --> 00:25:43.010
and beautiful.

00:25:43.010 --> 00:25:43.950
- I'm gonna tell you the truth.

00:25:43.950 --> 00:25:45.090
I want a bowl.

00:25:45.090 --> 00:25:46.710
I want an actual bowl.

00:25:46.710 --> 00:25:47.605
I don't want a basket.

00:25:47.605 --> 00:25:49.242
(laughing)

00:25:49.242 --> 00:25:50.220
- The baskets are beautiful.

00:25:50.220 --> 00:25:51.329
- Yeah, baskets are beautiful.

00:25:51.329 --> 00:25:52.162
- That's the point.

00:25:52.162 --> 00:25:54.850
I think that it shows a
very interesting utopia.

00:25:54.850 --> 00:25:58.740
- Actually, in the song
There's a Hole in the Bucket.

00:25:58.740 --> 00:25:59.573
- [Liu] Dear Liza.

00:25:59.573 --> 00:26:00.406
- Dear Liza.

00:26:01.460 --> 00:26:03.531
The solution to that is
to prepare it with straw.

00:26:03.531 --> 00:26:04.427
- That's right.

00:26:04.427 --> 00:26:06.709
- And I'm thinking, why
would straw hold water.

00:26:06.709 --> 00:26:07.590
(laughing)

00:26:07.590 --> 00:26:08.800
I'm just thinking that
when I heard that song.

00:26:08.800 --> 00:26:10.130
- If its woven tightly enough.

00:26:10.130 --> 00:26:10.963
- That's right.

00:26:10.963 --> 00:26:11.796
- You think so?

00:26:11.796 --> 00:26:13.870
- There are many waterproof straw things

00:26:13.870 --> 00:26:16.050
that the people, before
using metal or wood.

00:26:16.050 --> 00:26:16.950
- I was just thinking,

00:26:16.950 --> 00:26:20.150
is there any other way
you can plug this hole?

00:26:20.150 --> 00:26:22.256
Weld, I'm thinking technology, science.

00:26:22.256 --> 00:26:23.730
No, lets get straw, okay.

00:26:23.730 --> 00:26:25.800
- [Liu] Because the straw is right there.

00:26:25.800 --> 00:26:26.633
- I guess so.

00:26:26.633 --> 00:26:27.466
- Because if you gotta do the wood,

00:26:27.466 --> 00:26:29.400
you gotta hammer it
down or you gotta weld.

00:26:29.400 --> 00:26:30.502
- Okay, I just thought they would have

00:26:30.502 --> 00:26:32.720
a more permanent solution than just straw.

00:26:32.720 --> 00:26:34.410
And there's a hole in the
bucket, dear Liza, dear Liza.

00:26:34.410 --> 00:26:37.710
- All Georgey wanted was
to carry a little water

00:26:37.710 --> 00:26:38.620
from here to there.

00:26:38.620 --> 00:26:40.810
Why bring out vibranium for that?

00:26:40.810 --> 00:26:42.750
- And then he said, "How
will I cut the straw?"

00:26:42.750 --> 00:26:43.800
and they said, "With an ax."

00:26:43.800 --> 00:26:45.340
- [Liu] No no, with a knife.

00:26:45.340 --> 00:26:47.873
- Either, why do you have to, you know?

00:26:47.873 --> 00:26:49.378
I think the original song has an ax.

00:26:49.378 --> 00:26:50.470
- [Liu] An ax?

00:26:50.470 --> 00:26:51.500
- Yeah.

00:26:51.500 --> 00:26:53.070
- A little overkill for straw, I think.

00:26:53.070 --> 00:26:54.090
- No, that's what I'm saying!

00:26:54.090 --> 00:26:55.080
The whole song didn't make sense.

00:26:55.080 --> 00:26:56.140
I stopped listening to it.

00:26:56.140 --> 00:26:58.190
Okay, what were you saying Chuck?

00:26:58.190 --> 00:26:59.050
Last bit on invisibility
and then we can go

00:26:59.050 --> 00:27:00.300
to the nest question.

00:27:00.300 --> 00:27:03.500
- Last bit is that I
think that when we think

00:27:03.500 --> 00:27:07.330
about invisibility, we want
to be able to take it with us.

00:27:07.330 --> 00:27:10.530
The motion of being
invisible is more important

00:27:10.530 --> 00:27:13.300
than just being able to
render something not visible.

00:27:13.300 --> 00:27:14.133
- [Tyson] In a static plot.

00:27:14.133 --> 00:27:14.966
- In a static plot.

00:27:14.966 --> 00:27:17.450
And this is the value
that Harry Potter had.

00:27:17.450 --> 00:27:18.283
- [Liu] That's right.

00:27:18.283 --> 00:27:20.590
- You go wherever the cloaking is.

00:27:20.590 --> 00:27:22.880
- And so, that's really the
most important thing right now.

00:27:22.880 --> 00:27:25.186
The technology is not just to
make something not visible,

00:27:25.186 --> 00:27:27.940
but to make something not
visible as it moves around

00:27:27.940 --> 00:27:29.920
to everybody, from every point.

00:27:29.920 --> 00:27:32.870
- And in fact, the lab experiments
that you're describing,

00:27:32.870 --> 00:27:36.157
it is only invisible
from one exact direction.

00:27:36.157 --> 00:27:36.990
- [Liu] Correct.

00:27:36.990 --> 00:27:40.170
- Because the optics only
conspire for that point of view.

00:27:40.170 --> 00:27:41.685
Not from the side.

00:27:41.685 --> 00:27:42.850
- [Liu] That's where we start.

00:27:42.850 --> 00:27:44.080
Okay, we'll start there.

00:27:44.080 --> 00:27:44.913
- [Nice] All right, there ya go.

00:27:44.913 --> 00:27:46.550
Oh and by the way, one other thing,

00:27:46.550 --> 00:27:49.150
militaristically invisibility just means

00:27:49.150 --> 00:27:51.400
you're not reflecting a radar signal back.

00:27:51.400 --> 00:27:52.293
- Which we already have that,

00:27:52.293 --> 00:27:53.251
it's called stealth technology.

00:27:53.251 --> 00:27:54.084
- That's called stealth.

00:27:54.084 --> 00:27:55.850
So that is a kind of invisibility

00:27:55.850 --> 00:27:59.960
that accomplishes what the
Star Trek stealth things

00:27:59.960 --> 00:28:03.634
were doing, but in a militaristic sense.

00:28:03.634 --> 00:28:04.610
- All right, here we go.

00:28:04.610 --> 00:28:06.497
- Another Darpa project
that was, by the way.

00:28:06.497 --> 00:28:07.554
- [Nice] Stealth was a Darpa?

00:28:07.554 --> 00:28:08.630
- I think so.

00:28:08.630 --> 00:28:09.463
- [Nice] Nice.

00:28:09.463 --> 00:28:10.296
- Yeah we can verify that.

00:28:10.296 --> 00:28:12.470
We've got top crack researchers here.

00:28:12.470 --> 00:28:15.270
Yeah, Defense Advanced
Research Projects Agency.

00:28:15.270 --> 00:28:16.103
- [Nice] Are there any
other technologies--

00:28:16.103 --> 00:28:17.836
- And it was Lockheed that did that.

00:28:17.836 --> 00:28:20.260
- Yeah I do know that was Lockheed,

00:28:20.260 --> 00:28:23.960
but are there any other
technological applications

00:28:23.960 --> 00:28:27.030
for stealth technology, that's redundant.

00:28:27.030 --> 00:28:31.101
But are there any other
applications for stealth technology

00:28:31.101 --> 00:28:36.101
outside of absorbing radar
that we might find in life?

00:28:36.730 --> 00:28:37.563
- Well sure.

00:28:37.563 --> 00:28:38.396
- Well its not just absorbing radar.

00:28:38.396 --> 00:28:40.840
Radar absorbs all electromagnetic rays.

00:28:40.840 --> 00:28:42.144
So whatever might be--

00:28:42.144 --> 00:28:43.380
- Anything you're shooting at.

00:28:43.380 --> 00:28:44.280
- Shooting at, right.

00:28:44.280 --> 00:28:46.620
- And also, not all of
it, because some of it

00:28:46.620 --> 00:28:47.958
has to do with the shape of the plane too.

00:28:47.958 --> 00:28:49.410
- Everything has to do with the shape

00:28:49.410 --> 00:28:51.720
- That causes it to flow over.

00:28:51.720 --> 00:28:53.611
- Its the material, the shape.

00:28:53.611 --> 00:28:58.290
The V2 Bomber, which
looks like a bat plane.

00:28:58.290 --> 00:29:03.290
The shape is because, when
the radar signal does reflect,

00:29:03.330 --> 00:29:06.300
it doesn't reflect back to the
direction it was sent from.

00:29:06.300 --> 00:29:07.880
- So it doesn't reflect, it deflects.

00:29:07.880 --> 00:29:08.713
- It deflects.

00:29:08.713 --> 00:29:09.546
- Right.

00:29:09.546 --> 00:29:10.860
- And there are multiple reflections

00:29:10.860 --> 00:29:12.710
within the physical body.

00:29:12.710 --> 00:29:14.209
You don't want any of
them going back to where.

00:29:14.209 --> 00:29:16.427
They now have stealth Navy ships.

00:29:16.427 --> 00:29:18.200
(gasps)

00:29:18.200 --> 00:29:19.051
And you look at their shapes

00:29:19.051 --> 00:29:20.475
and its like, oh that's
a funky kind of shape.

00:29:20.475 --> 00:29:21.335
- Yeah, I've seen then.

00:29:21.335 --> 00:29:22.398
They are very cool looking.

00:29:22.398 --> 00:29:25.120
- And the masts have certain shapes

00:29:25.120 --> 00:29:28.810
just to minimize and reduce
the radar cross-section.

00:29:28.810 --> 00:29:33.810
- So it's just like,
"Oh it's a lost whale.

00:29:33.870 --> 00:29:36.140
Why is that lost whale
shooting a torpedo at us?"

00:29:36.140 --> 00:29:39.293
(heavy laughter)

00:29:39.293 --> 00:29:40.126
- All right, next.
- Cool.

00:29:40.126 --> 00:29:40.959
All Right, here we go.

00:29:40.959 --> 00:29:43.970
This is Yussel Lopez.

00:29:43.970 --> 00:29:44.803
- What's the first name?

00:29:44.803 --> 00:29:45.636
- Yussel.

00:29:45.636 --> 00:29:46.469
- Yussel, okay.

00:29:46.469 --> 00:29:48.461
- Yeah, or Yus-sel.

00:29:48.461 --> 00:29:49.689
(laughing)

00:29:49.689 --> 00:29:53.528
- Ussel was the name of--
- Is it Jessie or Jussie?

00:29:53.528 --> 00:29:54.691
- Yussel was the name of the character--

00:29:54.691 --> 00:29:58.020
- The character in Dune by Frank Herbert.

00:29:58.020 --> 00:30:01.547
It was the nickname given by
the Fremen to Paul Atreides.

00:30:01.547 --> 00:30:02.380
- Damn.

00:30:02.380 --> 00:30:03.460
- Ussel.

00:30:03.460 --> 00:30:04.293
- You are too good Chuck.

00:30:04.293 --> 00:30:05.431
I'm telling you man.

00:30:05.431 --> 00:30:06.680
- We are unworthy!

00:30:06.680 --> 00:30:07.513
- Yes.

00:30:07.513 --> 00:30:08.346
- When are unworthy.

00:30:08.346 --> 00:30:09.179
I saw the movie.

00:30:09.179 --> 00:30:10.820
The book was too think for me to read.

00:30:10.820 --> 00:30:12.320
I saw the movie I the 80s.

00:30:12.320 --> 00:30:13.660
- I saw the movie first.
- I gotta tell ya,

00:30:13.660 --> 00:30:15.606
I didn't read the book and
the movie was too damn long.

00:30:15.606 --> 00:30:16.955
(heavy laughter)

00:30:16.955 --> 00:30:17.980
- The movie should have been longer.

00:30:17.980 --> 00:30:19.983
- What I like was they have
their language translator.

00:30:19.983 --> 00:30:21.180
It's this thing you put
in front and you speak

00:30:21.180 --> 00:30:23.090
and out the other side.

00:30:23.090 --> 00:30:24.401
But now our iPhones do that.

00:30:24.401 --> 00:30:25.310
- I know!

00:30:25.310 --> 00:30:26.143
- [Tyson] Yeah, mm-hmm.

00:30:26.143 --> 00:30:26.976
- That's pretty wild.

00:30:26.976 --> 00:30:27.809
All right, here we go.

00:30:27.809 --> 00:30:32.090
This is what Yussel Lopez says.

00:30:32.090 --> 00:30:33.540
This is specifically for you Neil,

00:30:33.540 --> 00:30:37.867
but I'd like to know what Chuck
thinks the answer should be.

00:30:37.867 --> 00:30:40.980
"So a couple episodes
back, there was a reference

00:30:40.980 --> 00:30:44.370
to the Bat-Signal and a
guest jokingly mentioned

00:30:44.370 --> 00:30:46.658
that Neil should have a Neil signal.

00:30:46.658 --> 00:30:48.610
I'm curious Dr. Tyson.

00:30:48.610 --> 00:30:51.533
What would your Neil signal
look in the night sky be?

00:30:51.533 --> 00:30:53.397
- Oh that'd be, that's easy.

00:30:53.397 --> 00:30:56.360
Yeah, we already have one of these.

00:30:56.360 --> 00:31:01.360
There's a webpage on amazon
that sells sanctioned t-shirts

00:31:02.730 --> 00:31:04.900
that are designed by fans,
but inspired by my work

00:31:04.900 --> 00:31:06.500
or my words, or my image.

00:31:06.500 --> 00:31:08.867
But its all sanctioned and
fans get a fee and everything,

00:31:08.867 --> 00:31:10.160
and everybody's happy.

00:31:10.160 --> 00:31:15.110
And one of them is just simply
a silhouette of my hair line

00:31:16.269 --> 00:31:17.730
and a mustache.
(laughing)

00:31:17.730 --> 00:31:18.563
That's it.

00:31:18.563 --> 00:31:19.640
- [Nice] That's the Neil signal.

00:31:19.640 --> 00:31:22.340
- That's kinda me, right?

00:31:22.340 --> 00:31:23.173
- [Liu] Right.

00:31:23.173 --> 00:31:24.062
- Because I got this
sorta widows peak thing.

00:31:24.062 --> 00:31:26.580
- [Nice] Very Eddie
Munster, your hair line.

00:31:26.580 --> 00:31:28.397
- No but, Eddie Munster came to a point.

00:31:28.397 --> 00:31:29.949
- [Nice] Yours is natural though.

00:31:29.949 --> 00:31:33.343
- Yeah, and so it's a,

00:31:34.580 --> 00:31:37.830
and I think when I see that,
I think of myself abstracted.

00:31:37.830 --> 00:31:41.390
So then, you want a simple
iconography for the Bat-Signal.

00:31:41.390 --> 00:31:44.420
So the Neil-Signal, in case
you get stuck in an argument

00:31:44.420 --> 00:31:46.380
with a flat-earther and
you need help. (chuckles)

00:31:46.380 --> 00:31:49.323
- You just send the
hairline and the mustache.

00:31:50.781 --> 00:31:55.781
And Dr. Tyson will not show
up to your house (laughs)

00:31:56.540 --> 00:31:58.154
but he will solve the argument for you.

00:31:58.154 --> 00:31:59.570
That's cool man.

00:31:59.570 --> 00:32:00.403
- Yeah.

00:32:00.403 --> 00:32:01.860
- All right, Chuck, so what
do you think it would be?

00:32:01.860 --> 00:32:06.860
- I think it would be a ball in a box.

00:32:07.040 --> 00:32:09.697
The iconic structure of
the Haden Planetarium.

00:32:09.697 --> 00:32:11.063
- Oh the Rose Center for Earth and Space.

00:32:11.063 --> 00:32:12.250
- Ooh.

00:32:12.250 --> 00:32:14.490
- See, your silhouette is great

00:32:14.490 --> 00:32:16.271
but its a little too detailed.

00:32:16.271 --> 00:32:18.840
A little bit of clouds and you look

00:32:18.840 --> 00:32:20.130
like those are the clouds.

00:32:20.130 --> 00:32:20.963
- [Tyson] Yeah, you're right.

00:32:20.963 --> 00:32:23.220
- So you have to have basic shapes

00:32:23.220 --> 00:32:24.740
that are immediately recognizable.

00:32:24.740 --> 00:32:26.360
- And if we do that,

00:32:26.360 --> 00:32:28.860
because the Rose Center
for Earth and Space

00:32:28.860 --> 00:32:31.070
is a glass cube and a ball inside

00:32:31.070 --> 00:32:32.680
containing the Haden Planetarium.

00:32:32.680 --> 00:32:36.500
That iconography would
mean, if I'm not available,

00:32:36.500 --> 00:32:38.140
we get some of our other staff!

00:32:38.140 --> 00:32:39.656
- [Liu] There you go, tremendous talent.

00:32:39.656 --> 00:32:41.190
- Its just that you need an astrophysicist

00:32:41.190 --> 00:32:42.399
to help settle your argument.

00:32:42.399 --> 00:32:44.448
It doesn't have to be me. I
don't have unique knowledge

00:32:44.448 --> 00:32:45.281
in this world.

00:32:45.281 --> 00:32:47.395
- Now I'm going back to the
hair line and the mustache.

00:32:47.395 --> 00:32:48.228
- What, what?

00:32:48.228 --> 00:32:49.277
- Because you know somebody is just like,

00:32:49.277 --> 00:32:51.190
"Lets call Neil," and they put up the ball

00:32:51.190 --> 00:32:55.571
in the box, right, and all of
a sudden some intern shows up.

00:32:55.571 --> 00:32:57.400
(laughing)

00:32:57.400 --> 00:32:58.490
- Hey guys!

00:32:58.490 --> 00:33:00.897
Hey guys, what do ya need? (laughs)

00:33:00.897 --> 00:33:02.960
- But we got badass
interns, let me just say.

00:33:02.960 --> 00:33:03.793
- No, you do.

00:33:03.793 --> 00:33:04.626
This is true.

00:33:04.626 --> 00:33:06.410
All right, but I like the ball in the box,

00:33:06.410 --> 00:33:07.243
very, very thoughtful.

00:33:07.243 --> 00:33:08.650
- All right, hey Chuck,
what's the next question?

00:33:08.650 --> 00:33:09.733
- Okay, here we go.

00:33:10.840 --> 00:33:13.897
This is Daniel J. Lay and he says,

00:33:13.897 --> 00:33:16.010
"This is for Chuck and Neil.

00:33:16.010 --> 00:33:17.800
What superpower would you like to have

00:33:17.800 --> 00:33:22.318
that could be theoretically
enabled by gene editing.

00:33:22.318 --> 00:33:24.605
(everyone exclaims excitedly)

00:33:24.605 --> 00:33:26.140
- Good one, very X-Files.

00:33:26.140 --> 00:33:27.390
I mean, very X-Man.

00:33:27.390 --> 00:33:28.223
- Yes!

00:33:28.223 --> 00:33:29.660
- On that one, I have an answer.

00:33:29.660 --> 00:33:30.830
Chuck, do you have an answer?

00:33:30.830 --> 00:33:31.663
- I do.

00:33:31.663 --> 00:33:32.496
- We don't have time to give it.

00:33:32.496 --> 00:33:33.329
- Agh.

00:33:33.329 --> 00:33:34.162
- We gotta go to break. (chuckles)

00:33:34.162 --> 00:33:35.100
- All right, well go to break.

00:33:35.100 --> 00:33:37.870
- Take a break, and when we come back,

00:33:37.870 --> 00:33:40.630
Chuck and I will argue about
what genes we will edit

00:33:40.630 --> 00:33:45.630
to try to get some kind
of superhero superpowers

00:33:45.750 --> 00:33:47.890
when Star Talk returns.
(electronic music pings)

00:33:47.890 --> 00:33:49.870
This episode of Star
Talk is brought to you

00:33:49.870 --> 00:33:51.883
by Curiosity Stream.

00:33:52.980 --> 00:33:54.386
Star Talk, we're back.

00:33:54.386 --> 00:33:55.800
Tyson here.

00:33:55.800 --> 00:33:57.011
Chuck Liu there.

00:33:57.011 --> 00:33:58.100
Chuck nice there.

00:33:58.100 --> 00:33:59.040
(giggles)

00:33:59.040 --> 00:34:00.932
Charles Liu, Chuck Nice.

00:34:00.932 --> 00:34:02.010
Chuck Liu and Charles Nice.

00:34:02.010 --> 00:34:02.843
- That's right.

00:34:02.843 --> 00:34:03.930
(laughs)

00:34:03.930 --> 00:34:05.950
- Okay, you tweet don't you, occasionally?

00:34:05.950 --> 00:34:06.969
- @chuckliu,

00:34:06.969 --> 00:34:09.187
at C-H-U-C-K-L-I-U.
- See, it does use Chuck.

00:34:09.187 --> 00:34:10.580
- Chuck Liu, L-I-U.

00:34:10.580 --> 00:34:12.980
- Yeah, that's because
Charles was already taken, so.

00:34:12.980 --> 00:34:13.813
- Oh that's too bad.

00:34:13.813 --> 00:34:14.740
- Well no, its actually better.

00:34:14.740 --> 00:34:16.740
It reminds me of my college roots.

00:34:16.740 --> 00:34:18.365
- [Tyson] Okay, and you are?

00:34:18.365 --> 00:34:19.885
- @chucknicecomedy.

00:34:19.885 --> 00:34:21.110
That's my name and what I do.

00:34:21.110 --> 00:34:22.780
You actually have to tell
people you're a comic.

00:34:22.780 --> 00:34:23.613
- Well you know, I'm not that good at it.

00:34:23.613 --> 00:34:24.554
- [Tyson] That means your
tweets were not-- (chuckles)

00:34:24.554 --> 00:34:26.281
- I'm not that good at it.

00:34:26.281 --> 00:34:27.671
- Just in case you forgot.

00:34:27.671 --> 00:34:29.820
(loud chuckling)

00:34:29.820 --> 00:34:30.920
- Its like, "What is this tweet?

00:34:30.920 --> 00:34:31.901
Oh he's a comedian!"

00:34:31.901 --> 00:34:34.320
- And by by the way, it
was originally Chuck Nice,

00:34:34.320 --> 00:34:37.017
black comic, but I figured
okay, they'll figure it out.

00:34:37.017 --> 00:34:38.274
(laughing)

00:34:38.274 --> 00:34:42.330
- He also has another Twitter handle

00:34:42.330 --> 00:34:43.556
that he doesn't publicize so much.

00:34:43.556 --> 00:34:45.980
It's @ChuckNiceGoGoDancer.

00:34:45.980 --> 00:34:46.840
- Is that right?

00:34:46.840 --> 00:34:47.675
- No that's not right.

00:34:47.675 --> 00:34:49.326
(loud laughing)

00:34:49.326 --> 00:34:50.580
- You know, I thought I saw that.

00:34:50.580 --> 00:34:51.413
That wasn't you?

00:34:51.413 --> 00:34:52.246
- That was just for you.

00:34:52.246 --> 00:34:53.705
- Oh, I'm so sorry.

00:34:53.705 --> 00:34:55.270
(laughing)

00:34:55.270 --> 00:34:56.692
- All right, so read this question again

00:34:56.692 --> 00:34:57.660
where we left off.
- Okay, so right

00:34:57.660 --> 00:35:00.110
before we went to the break, Daniel J. Lay

00:35:00.110 --> 00:35:03.527
asked a great question
for Neil and Charles.

00:35:03.527 --> 00:35:05.900
"What superpower would you like to have

00:35:05.900 --> 00:35:08.194
that could be theoretically enabled

00:35:08.194 --> 00:35:10.590
(excitedly) by gene editing!

00:35:10.590 --> 00:35:11.903
- Okay, Chuck you go first.
- Ooh.

00:35:13.270 --> 00:35:14.320
Charles you go first.

00:35:15.560 --> 00:35:18.170
- I would say, and I know this
might seem a little goofy,

00:35:18.170 --> 00:35:22.020
but with gene editing, the best
thing that I could hope for

00:35:22.020 --> 00:35:26.150
for myself is that my genes
make it so that I will stay

00:35:26.150 --> 00:35:29.940
as mentally and physical in perfect health

00:35:29.940 --> 00:35:30.773
as long as I live.

00:35:30.773 --> 00:35:31.870
- That's not a superpower.

00:35:31.870 --> 00:35:34.210
- It is, immortality!

00:35:34.210 --> 00:35:35.920
But its healthy immortality.

00:35:35.920 --> 00:35:38.370
- A superpower is somebody's in distress

00:35:38.370 --> 00:35:39.470
and you go help them.

00:35:39.470 --> 00:35:40.730
- Yes, if I am immortal--

00:35:40.730 --> 00:35:43.610
- And he's like this, "I'm
gonna stand here and watch you."

00:35:43.610 --> 00:35:44.443
- Watch you die,

00:35:44.443 --> 00:35:45.303
while I live forever.
- While I live forever.

00:35:45.303 --> 00:35:46.703
(chuckling)

00:35:46.703 --> 00:35:47.536
- Charles.

00:35:47.536 --> 00:35:50.060
- If I am immortal, then I
will have the opportunity

00:35:50.060 --> 00:35:55.040
to learn all the medical
technology, all of the information

00:35:55.040 --> 00:35:57.010
over the centuries, the millennia.

00:35:57.010 --> 00:35:58.900
And that way, I will
be able to help people

00:35:58.900 --> 00:36:01.570
not by being some crazy guy in a costume.

00:36:01.570 --> 00:36:03.390
- Wait, medical knowledge
over the centuries includes

00:36:03.390 --> 00:36:05.327
like bloodletting, and this sort of thing.

00:36:05.327 --> 00:36:07.920
- Yes, well it depends on
whether that bloodletting

00:36:07.920 --> 00:36:08.960
is worth keeping or not.

00:36:08.960 --> 00:36:09.793
- Okay.

00:36:09.793 --> 00:36:12.750
- But for example, these
days medical people

00:36:12.750 --> 00:36:14.580
actually use leeches to help get rid

00:36:14.580 --> 00:36:16.140
of hematomas.
- Yeah, get rid of bad blood.

00:36:16.140 --> 00:36:17.190
- [Liu] That's right.

00:36:18.175 --> 00:36:19.628
- Excuse me, hematoma, excuse me.

00:36:19.628 --> 00:36:20.932
(laughing)

00:36:20.932 --> 00:36:23.900
- I want to make it so
that I have great value

00:36:23.900 --> 00:36:26.700
to society without my having to stand out

00:36:26.700 --> 00:36:29.760
as some sort of a beacon or a flag post.

00:36:29.760 --> 00:36:30.593
Does that make sense?

00:36:30.593 --> 00:36:32.855
So I think it's tremendous.
- No! But all right.

00:36:32.855 --> 00:36:34.942
- Okay Charles, no one is
making a movie about that.

00:36:34.942 --> 00:36:35.940
(chuckles)

00:36:35.940 --> 00:36:37.380
- Maybe not.

00:36:37.380 --> 00:36:39.030
Oh, well there was The Fountain.

00:36:39.030 --> 00:36:40.840
- It's the man who reads!

00:36:40.840 --> 00:36:41.673
- [Tyson] The fountain of youth?

00:36:41.673 --> 00:36:44.430
- Yeah, that was the one
Darren Aronofsky put together,

00:36:44.430 --> 00:36:47.280
starring Hugh Jackman and Rachel Weisz.

00:36:47.280 --> 00:36:49.385
- Huge Ackman, that's the
brother of Hugh Jackman?

00:36:49.385 --> 00:36:52.040
(laughing)

00:36:52.040 --> 00:36:53.350
- All right Neils, what was yours?

00:36:53.350 --> 00:36:55.280
So Charles is going to live forever.

00:36:55.280 --> 00:36:56.960
- I thought about this actually,

00:36:56.960 --> 00:37:01.190
and if gene editing is what is allowed,

