00:42:03.927 --> 00:42:05.770
"My friend and I got
into a space discussion

00:42:05.770 --> 00:42:09.190
about future star ship design.

00:42:09.190 --> 00:42:12.730
Sci-fi franchises like to make
use of the simulated gravity

00:42:12.730 --> 00:42:14.570
on star ships and space station's

00:42:14.570 --> 00:42:17.004
by the way of centrifugal force

00:42:17.004 --> 00:42:18.520
or centripetal force.
- Rotating sections.

00:42:18.520 --> 00:42:20.295
- Which is rotation section,

00:42:20.295 --> 00:42:25.295
in a habitat section, and
we all know what they are.

00:42:27.170 --> 00:42:29.710
As earliest evidence in, what was it,

00:42:29.710 --> 00:42:31.300
2001: A Space Odyssey, right?

00:42:31.300 --> 00:42:34.020
Okay, "Would it be
realistic to use this idea

00:42:34.020 --> 00:42:37.350
for long term space travel?"

00:42:37.350 --> 00:42:38.183
- Yep.

00:42:38.183 --> 00:42:39.016
- Yep.

00:42:39.016 --> 00:42:39.980
- Yep.

00:42:39.980 --> 00:42:40.813
And next question.

00:42:40.813 --> 00:42:41.646
(laughing)

00:42:41.646 --> 00:42:42.479
Von, there's your answer.

00:42:42.479 --> 00:42:43.500
- The point is--

00:42:43.500 --> 00:42:44.333
- It works!

00:42:44.333 --> 00:42:46.640
- There's all this research
on the medical effects

00:42:46.640 --> 00:42:48.160
of zero-G.

00:42:48.160 --> 00:42:51.880
None of which would be necessary
if our long term spaceships

00:42:51.880 --> 00:42:55.010
had rotating habitats,
and you don't have to be

00:42:55.010 --> 00:42:57.320
in there 100% of the
time, but you go in there

00:42:57.320 --> 00:42:59.511
to get your sea legs back, you know?

00:42:59.511 --> 00:43:01.350
And you lift weights, you do whatever.

00:43:01.350 --> 00:43:04.027
- And your bone density stay
the same and everything.

00:43:04.027 --> 00:43:05.657
So now, how fast would it have to spin

00:43:05.657 --> 00:43:07.300
to give you one-G,

00:43:07.300 --> 00:43:09.210
which is where we are.
- It depends on the radius.

00:43:09.210 --> 00:43:10.043
- [Nice] So the radius of the system?

00:43:10.043 --> 00:43:10.876
- Entirely!

00:43:10.876 --> 00:43:12.234
- So the smaller the radius the--

00:43:12.234 --> 00:43:13.203
- [Nice] The faster
it's gonna spin, right.

00:43:13.203 --> 00:43:14.036
- Correct.

00:43:14.036 --> 00:43:14.996
- And the larger the radius,
the slower it can go.

00:43:14.996 --> 00:43:15.829
- So you want to make
it as big as possible.

00:43:15.829 --> 00:43:16.950
- As big as possible.

00:43:16.950 --> 00:43:18.220
- Right, and remember in space,

00:43:18.220 --> 00:43:19.730
since there's no air resistance--

00:43:19.730 --> 00:43:20.770
- But why do you have to
make it as big as possible?

00:43:20.770 --> 00:43:21.640
Who cares?

00:43:21.640 --> 00:43:22.473
It doesn't matter.

00:43:22.473 --> 00:43:25.600
- If it's small, then
your inner ear has issues

00:43:25.600 --> 00:43:26.433
with the rotation.

00:43:26.433 --> 00:43:30.570
- If you are large relative
to the size of the thing.

00:43:30.570 --> 00:43:33.400
But I'm saying, if it's
still big relative to you,

00:43:33.400 --> 00:43:34.870
then the size doesn't matter.

00:43:34.870 --> 00:43:35.703
- But its gotta get to that size.

00:43:35.703 --> 00:43:38.120
- But I'm sure there's a particular size

00:43:38.120 --> 00:43:39.750
that's gonna get me the one-G, right?

00:43:39.750 --> 00:43:42.340
For your size.
- No, any size.

00:43:42.340 --> 00:43:43.760
In fact, do this, right.

00:43:43.760 --> 00:43:44.593
- [Nice] Go ahead.

00:43:44.593 --> 00:43:46.501
- Take a bucket of water
and swing it fast enough

00:43:46.501 --> 00:43:48.010
